        <h1>Chapter 1: Distribution, Indicators & Applications</h1>
      <p>
        The distribution describes how values are arranged across their range. This chapter focuses on
        <em>profiles</em> of distributions, their <em>indicators</em> used to describe them,
        and the real-world <em>applications</em> where these ideas are useful.
      </p>
      <h2>Distribution profiles</h2>
        <ul>The "shape" of a distribution gives a quick visual summary of how values are concentrated, where tails lie,
        and whether there are multiple peaks:
<li>Symmetrical / Normal (Bell-shaped):Balanced on both sides; mean ≈ median ≈ mode.</li>
<li>Right-Skew (Positive) : Most values at lower side, long tail to the right.</li>
<li>Left-Skew (Negative): Most values at higher side, tail to the left.</li>
<li>Bimodal / Multimodal:Two groups or clusters in the same dataset.</li>
        </ul>

<h2>Shapes</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Skewness</strong> — numeric measure of asymmetry (positive = right tail).</li>
            <li><strong>Kurtosis</strong> — measure of "tailedness" or peakedness relative to normal.</li>
            <li><strong>Outliers</strong> — extreme values that differ markedly from others (often detected with IQR).</li>
          </ul>
<br>
      <h2>Key Indicators</h2>
      <ul>Indicators summarize position, spread, and shape of the distribution:
          
            <li><strong>Mean</strong> — arithmetic average (sensitive to outliers).</li>
            <li><strong>Median</strong> — middle value after ordering (robust to outliers).</li>
            <li><strong>Mode</strong> — most frequent value (useful for categories).</li>
            <li><strong>Range</strong> — max − min.</li>
            <li><strong>Variance</strong> — average squared deviation from the mean.</li>
            <li><strong>Standard Deviation (SD)</strong> — square root of variance (same units as data).</li>
            <li><strong>Interquartile Range (IQR)</strong> — difference between 75th and 25th percentiles (middle 50%).</li>
          </ul>
