      <h2> Applications — How to use Profiles & Indicators</h2>
	<br>
      <p><strong>Education Score</strong> : 
        Teachers examine test score distributions. A <strong>right-skew</strong> may indicate many low scores and a few high performers;
        a <strong>bimodal</strong> distribution can reveal two student groups and suggest targeted instruction.</p>
      <p><strong>Business & Marketing:</strong> most customers make small purchases; a few are big spenders.
        Use median revenue to report typical customer value and detect top-performing clients via outliers.</p>   
      <p><strong>Healthcare</strong> : 
        Lab results or recovery times may be skewed. Using median and IQR provides robust summaries when outliers exist.
        Detecting unusual outliers helps flag cases for clinical review.
      </p>
     <p><strong>Public Policy & Economics</strong>:
        Income distribution is usually right-skewed; policymakers prefer median income (not mean) to discuss typical household conditions.
        Age distributions guide resource planning for schools and eldercare.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Sports & Performance</strong>
        Coaches look at distribution of times or scores. Low variance indicates consistent performance; a long tail may indicate a few exceptional results.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Note:</strong> Always pair a visual (histogram or box plot) with numeric indicators (mean/median, SD/IQR, skewness) — visuals reveal shape; numbers quantify it.
      </p> 
      <h2>Choosing Appropriate Indicators — Quick Guide</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Symmetrical distribution:</strong> mean and standard deviation are fine.</li>
        <li><strong>Skewed distribution:</strong> prefer median and IQR (robust measures).</li>
        <li><strong>Presence of outliers:</strong> use median & IQR; examine raw data points to decide if outliers are errors or real observations.</li>
        <li><strong>Bimodal data:</strong> report modes or consider splitting the dataset into meaningful groups before summarizing.</li>
      </ul>


