<h1>Chapter 2: Dendrogram Clustering </h1>
<p>Dendrogram clustering organizes data into groups using a hierarchical tree. It does not require predefining the number of clusters. 
Instead, clusters form stepwise by merging similar data points. The dendrogram’s horizontal axis shows samples, while the vertical axis shows the distance between merged clusters. By analyzing it, you can choose the number of clusters that best fit your data.</p>


<h2>Principle of the Dendrogram</h2> 
<ul> 
<li>A <strong>dendrogram</strong> is a branching diagram that visualizes how groups are merged (agglomerative) or split (divisive).</li> 
<li>Data points start as individual clusters, which are gradually merged based on their similarity or distance.</li> 
<li>Each branch point in the tree shows when clusters combine; the height indicates the distance at which they merge.</li> 
<li>Cutting the dendrogram at different heights produces different cluster groupings.</li> </ul> 
<h2>Methods of Hierarchical Clustering</h2> 
<ul> 
<li><strong>Agglomerative clustering</strong> — a bottom-up approach. Each data point is its own cluster. At each step, the two closest clusters are merged.</li> 
<li><strong>Divisive clustering</strong> — a top-down approach. All data points start in one cluster, which is split recursively into smaller clusters.</li> </ul>
<h2>Linkage criteria</h2>
<p>single, complete, average, Ward can be used to define how distances between clusters are calculated.The choice affects cluster shape and structure:<ul> 
<li>Single linkage: Distance between the closest points of two clusters. Creates "chain-like" clusters but is sensitive to noise and outliers.</li>
<li>Complete linkage: Distance between the farthest points. Produces more compact, spherical clusters but can be distorted by outliers.</li>
<li>Average linkage: Average distance between all pairs of points in two clusters. Balances single and complete linkage effects, producing moderate cluster sizes.</li>
<li>Ward’s linkage: Minimizes the total within-cluster variance after merging. Tends to create balanced and compact clusters, widely used in practice.</li>
</ul> 

