 <h2> Application</h2><br>
<p> <strong>Bioinformatics :</strong>
    Dendrograms are extensively used to group genes or samples by similarity in gene-expression profiles (microarray / RNA-seq), revealing co-regulated gene sets and sample subtypes; the classic example is the clustering work by Eisen et al. that visualized genome-wide expression as dendrograms alongside heatmaps. 
    <a href="https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.95.25.14863" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Eisen et al., PNAS (1998)</a>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Healthcare : </strong>
    Hierarchical clustering and dendrograms are used to identify clinically meaningful patient subgroups (stratification) from electronic health records or multi-omics data, which can reveal prognostic groups or treatment-responsive cohorts; recent studies demonstrate robust, explainable pipelines for clinical subtyping. 
    <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10854470/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Werner et al., 2023 (explainable hierarchical clustering)</a>; 
  </p>

  <p><strong>Ecology & Biodiversity : </strong>
    Ecologists apply dendrogram clustering to group sites or species by community composition (presence/abundance), enabling classification of habitat types, detection of assemblages, and tracking changes in biodiversity across gradients.  
    <a href="https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecs2.4589" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pang et al., Ecological Applications (2023)</a>. 
  </p>

  <p><strong>Image analysis : </strong>
    Bottom-up hierarchical clustering of image regions produces dendrograms that guide multilevel image segmentation and cosegmentation; modern approaches combine hierarchical clustering with deep features to generate interpretable segmentation trees.  
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.09949" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HCFormer — hierarchical clustering for segmentation (arXiv, 2022)</a>.
  </p>

   <p><strong>Materials & Polymers</strong>
    Researchers use dendrograms to group material compositions, processing conditions, or measured property profiles (e.g., mechanical tests) to detect formulations with similar performance and to guide selection of candidate blends for further testing.  
    Representative example: hierarchical approaches applied to polymer blend evaluation and property clustering. 
<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polymer.2015.07.025" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Polymer, 2015</a>.
  </p>


