  <h1>Chapter 3: Clustering — Overview and Types</h1>
  <h2>Common Clustering Types</h2>
  <p>
    Clustering groups data points into clusters so that points within a cluster are similar, and points from different clusters are dissimilar.<br>

    Additional clustering approaches include distribution-based, which models clusters via probability distributions (e.g., Gaussian Mixture Models); 
and fuzzy clustering, which allows overlapping cluster membership, useful for ambiguous data.</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Centroid-based clustering:</strong>  
    This method groups data points around central points called centroids, which represent the mean position of all points in the cluster.  
    The most common algorithm is <em>K-Means</em>, which starts by initializing k centroids and iteratively assigns data points to the nearest centroid, then recalculates centroids based on current assignments.  
    It is fast and efficient for large datasets but assumes clusters are roughly spherical and requires specifying the number of clusters (k) beforehand.  
  </li>

  <li>
    <strong>Density-based clustering:</strong>  
    These algorithms identify clusters as areas of high point density separated by regions of low density.  
   Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise defines clusters based on two parameters: the radius (ε) for neighborhood search and the minimum number of points (MinPts) needed to form a dense region.  
    DBSCAN can find clusters of arbitrary shape and is robust to noise by marking sparse points as outliers.  
    This makes it well suited for real-world data with noise and varying cluster shapes, but it may struggle with varying density clusters.
  </li>

  <li>
    <strong>Hierarchical clustering:</strong>  
    Builds a tree of nested clusters called a dendrogram, either by agglomerative (bottom-up) or divisive (top-down) approaches.  
    Agglomerative methods start with each data point as a cluster and successively merge nearest clusters, while divisive methods split clusters recursively.  
    This approach does not require predefining the number of clusters, making it flexible and interpretable.  
    However, it can be computationally intensive for large datasets and sensitive to the choice of linkage method and distance metric.
  </li>

  <li>
    <strong>Distribution-based and fuzzy clustering methods:</strong>  
    Distribution-based clustering assumes data is generated by a mixture of probability distributions, such as Gaussian Mixture Models, which estimate cluster parameters using statistical methods like Expectation-Maximization.  
    These methods model uncertainty and overlapping clusters, providing soft assignments of data points.  
    Fuzzy clustering, like Fuzzy C-Means, allows points to belong to multiple clusters with varying memberships, which is useful when cluster boundaries are ambiguous.  
    Such approaches can capture complex data structures but tend to be more computationally expensive.
  </li>
</ul>

