  <h1>Chapter 5: Principal Component Analysis (PCA) — Variable reduction</h1>
  <p>
    Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to reduce dimensionality of large datasets.  
    It transforms correlated variables into a smaller number of uncorrelated variables called principal components <strong>[Ck]</strong>, which retain most of the variance in the original data.</p>
  <h2>Method and Principles</h2>
  <p>
    PCA identifies new axes, called eigenvectors, corresponding to directions of maximum variance in the data, ordered by decreasing eigenvalues representing explained variance.  
    The first principal component explains the largest variability; subsequent components explain the remaining variance orthogonal to previous ones.  
    By selecting the first few components, PCA reduces data dimension while minimizing information loss.  
    This requires centering and scaling data, then computing the covariance matrix and its eigen decomposition to find principal components.
  </p>
<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
  <mi>C</mi><msub><mi>k</mi></msub>
  <mo>=</mo>
  <mrow>
    <mo>&#x2211;</mo> <!-- symbole somme -->
  </mrow>
  <msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub>
  <msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub>
</math>
<ul>With:
  <li><b>C<sub>k</sub></b>: k<sup>th</sup> principal component</li>
  <li><b>X<sub>j</sub></b>: j<sup>th</sup> centered and scaled variable</li>
  <li><b>U<sub>kj</sub></b>: coefficient of the associated eigenvector. &Sigma;Ui=1 because it uses centered and scaled variable.</li>
</ul>

  <h2>Usage in Data Analysis</h2>
  <p>
    PCA is commonly used for visualization of high-dimensional data, exploratory analysis, noise reduction, and feature extraction.  
    It prepares data for subsequent methods such as clustering or regression by simplifying variable space.  
    The correlation circle is a common PCA plot that shows variable contributions and relationships on principal component axes.
  </p>
