<h1>Chapter 6 : Plackett-Burman Designs</h1>
<p>
Plackett-Burman designs are efficient fractional factorial experimental designs mainly used to <strong>screen and identify</strong> the most influential variables among a large number of factors.  
These designs require only a small number of experimental runs relative to the number of variables, making them ideal for early-stage experimental screening (<a href="https://www.sctunisie.org/pdf/JSCT_v13-22.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JSCT, 2022</a>).

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
Plackett-Burman designs are two-level fractional factorial plans with run sizes modulo 4 (12, 20, 24, 28, 32, etc.) from Hadamard orthogonal matrices (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s2-35.1.241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paley, 1933</a>). 
They allow estimation of main effects assuming interactions are negligible.  
For <em>N</em> runs, up to <em>N-1</em> variables can be screened efficiently.  
These screening pinpoints the significant factors while minimizing the number of experiments.
</p>
<h2>Advantages</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Highly efficient for screening a large number of variables with few experiments, saving time & resources.</li>
  <li>Orthogonality ensures that the estimates of main effects are independent, reducing confounding between factors.</li>
  <li>Supports statistical analysis, allowing identification of significant factors and effect estimation.</li>
  <li>Simple two-level design makes it easy to implement and analyze, even for non-expert experimenters.</li>
  <li>Flexible run sizes allow adaptation to different numbers of variables and experimental constraints.</li>
</ul>
<br>
<h2>Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Focuses only on main effects. If interactions are significant, results may be misleading.</li>
  <li>Cannot estimate interactions directly; additional experiments are needed to explore them.</li>
  <li>Not suitable for experiments where curvature or non-linear effects are important.</li>
  <li>Designs are limited to certain run sizes (multiples of 4), which may not match the exact number of factors.</li>
  <li>Interpretation requires careful consideration: false positives can occur if noise are present.</li>
</ul>


