<h1>Chapter 7 : Design of Experiments (DoE)</h1>
<p>
Design of Experiments (DoE) is a  structured statistical approach used to plan, conduct, analyze, and interpret controlled tests.  
Its primary purpose is to understand and quantify the effects that multiple input variables (factors) have on one or more output variables (responses). 
</p>
<p>
In fractional factorial designs, the <strong>design matrix</strong> represents the layout of experimental runs, listing combinations of factor levels for each trial.  
Each row corresponds to one experimental run, and each column to a factor or an interaction term. Matrices are often derived from Hadamard matrices, which are orthogonal arrays 
of ±1 entries ensuring clear estimation of main effects.  
</p>

<p>
For <strong>3<sup>N-k</sup></strong> designs, factors have three levels (generally coded as -1, 0, and +1). The matrices use complex algebraic constructions to maintain orthogonality and balance among factor levels, enabling accurate effect estimation with fewer runs while considering nonlinear effects.
</p>

<h2>2<sup>N-k</sup> Fractional Factorial Designs</h2>
<p>
Fractional factorial designs 2<sup>(n-k)</sup> are used to reduce the number of experimental runs when studying multiple factors, each at <strong>two levels, min (-1) and max(+1)</strong> by studying only a fraction of the full factorial.  
These designs rely on the assumption that higher-order (x1x2x3x4 ...) interactions are negligible, allowing estimation of main effects and low-order interactions with fewer experiments.
For example, a full factorial on 7 factors (<span style="font-size:0.8em">2<sup>7</sup></span>=128 runs) can be replaced by a <span style="font-size:0.8em">2<sup>(7-4)</sup></span> 8 runs).
</p>
<h2>3<sup>N-k</sup> Fractional Factorial Designs</h2>
<p>
Fractional factorial designs 3<sup>N-k</sup> extend the concept to factors with three levels each, allowing efficient experimentation when multiple numeric factors must be studied over
<strong> low-medium-high (-1;0;+1)</strong> settings.  
These designs capture nonlinear effects and quadratic relationships better than 2-level designs but require more runs.
</p>

