<h1>Chapter 8: Mixture Designs (2ᵏ and 3ᵏ)</h1>
<p>
Mixture designs are a special class of experimental designs used when the response depends on the relative proportions of components in a blend rather than their absolute amounts.  
Unlike factorial designs where factors can vary independently, in mixture experiments the sum of the component proportions is constrained (usually to 1 or 100%).  
They are widely applied in fields such as chemistry, food science, pharmaceuticals, and materials engineering where formulations involve combining several ingredients (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00949655.2016.1156684" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cornell, 2016</a>).  
</p>
<h2>Methodology</h2>
<ul>
Mixture designs can be structured as 2ᵏ or 3ᵏ factorial-type experiments, where <em>k</em> is the number of components:  

  <li><strong>2ᵏ mixture design:</strong> Each component is tested at two proportions (low and high). This approach is efficient for preliminary screening to identify main effects of ingredients on the response.</li>
  <li><strong>3ᵏ mixture design:</strong> Each component is tested at three proportions (low, medium, high). This design captures curvature and interaction effects, making it suitable for optimization once key ingredients are identified.</li>
<p>
Because mixture designs must satisfy the constant-sum constraint, special coordinate systems (such as simplex-lattice or simplex-centroid arrangements) 
are used to define experimental points (<a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section5/pri56.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST/SEMATECH Handbook</a>). </p> 
</ul>
<h2>Why 2-Level and 3-Level Designs?</h2>
<p>
Two-level (2ᵏ) mixture designs are useful for <strong>screening</strong>. They require fewer runs and can quickly indicate which components are influential.  
However, they cannot capture nonlinear blending effects or synergistic/antagonistic interactions. 
<br><br>Three-level (3ᵏ) mixture designs provide greater flexibility by including intermediate levels, which makes it possible to model <strong>curvature</strong> 
and more accurately describe how component proportions influence the response.  </p><p>
Practitioners often start with a 2ᵏ mixture design for factor screening, then move to a 3ᵏ design for fine-tuning and optimization.  
</p>
